ardless of the inputs.\n\nBy completing the final column in the truth table below, we obtain the following:\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\,P∧Q\\,$ | $\\,¬P∨Q\\,$ | $\\,(P∧Q)∧(¬P∨Q)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n\nAccording to the table, $(P∧Q)∧(¬P∨Q)$ \n$\\text{isn't}$\nalways false. Therefore, it \n$\\text{isn't}$\na contradiction." 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412472 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412473 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412477 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412479 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412488 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412489 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412491 1069 "q-216769" 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412491 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412491 1071 "Select the correct options in the truth table below and use this to determine whether $(¬A∧B)∧(A∧¬B)$ is a contradiction." 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412491 1072 #uuid "47f2ef05-063f-5c83-82af-b14b43aa298e" 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412491 1074 "An expression is a contradiction if it is always false, regardless of the inputs.\n\nBy completing the final column in the truth table below, we obtain the following:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,¬A∧B\\,$ | $\\,A∧¬B\\,$ | $\\,(¬A∧B)∧(A∧¬B)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n\nAccording to the table, $(¬A∧B)∧(A∧¬B)$ \n$\\text{is}$\nalways false. Therefore, it \n$\\text{is}$\na contradiction." 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412492 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412475 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412492 1069 "q-216907" 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412492 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412492 1071 "Simplify the expression $¬[(¬X∨¬Y)∨(¬Y∧Y)].$" 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412492 1072 #uuid "480f94bc-0561-588e-b570-1657983e06e2" 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412492 1074 "Let's simplify the expression using the properties of logical operations:\n\n- First, we use the fact that the conjunction of a statement and its negation is a contradiction:\n\n$$\n¬[(¬X∨¬Y)∨(¬Y∧Y)]≡¬[(¬X∨¬Y)∨\\text{c}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we use the fact that the disjunction of a statement and a contradiction equals the original statement:\n\n$$\n¬[(¬X∨¬Y)∨\\text{c}]≡¬(¬X∨¬Y)\n$$\n\n- Then, we use a De Morgan's law:\n\n$$\n¬(¬X∨¬Y)≡¬¬(X∧Y)\n$$\n\n- Finally,  we simplify the double negation:\n\n$$\n¬¬(X∧Y)≡X∧Y\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n¬[(¬X∨¬Y)∨(¬Y∧Y)]≡X∧Y\n$$" 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412493 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412487 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412493 1069 "q-107597" 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412493 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412493 1071 "Simplify the expression $(A∧B)∨(A∧¬B).$" 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412493 1072 #uuid "ce194f41-6fc2-5669-9a9a-5765a4bee419" 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412493 1074 "Let's simplify the expression using the properties of logical operations:\n\n- First,  we use the distributive law (conjunction over disjunction):\n\n$$\n(A∧B)∨(A∧¬B)≡A∧(B∨¬B)\n$$\n\n- Next,  we use the fact that the disjunction of an expression and its negation is a tautology:\n\n$$\nA∧(B∨¬B)≡A∧\\text{t}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we use the fact that the conjunction of an expression and a tautology gives the initial expression:\n\n$$\nA∧\\text{t}≡A\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(A∧B)∨(A∧¬B)≡A.\n$$" 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412494 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412481 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412494 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412484 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412494 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412485 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412494 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412486 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412494 1069 "q-218619" 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412494 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412494 1071 "If $P$ is a statement, $\\text{t}$ is a tautology, and $\\text{c}$ is a contradiction, complete the following table of logical equivalences." 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412494 1072 #uuid "e8b03653-3dbc-58b5-96b9-61df0ae25ec2" 13194139534155 17592186045417 true] [17592186412494 1074 "Recall the following properties of tautologies and contradictions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A∨\\text{t}=\\text{t}\\, &  & A∧\\text{t} & =A \\\\ A∨\\text{c}=A\\, &  & A∧\\text{c} & =\\text{c} \\\\ \\text{t}∨\\text{t}=\\text{t}\\, &  & \\text{t}∧\\text{t} & =\\text{t} \\\\ \\text{t}∨\\text{c}=\\text{t}\\, &  & \\text{t}∧\\text{c} & =\\text{c} \\\\ \\text{c}∨\\text{t}=\\text{t}\\, &  & \\text{c}∧\\text{t} & =\\text{c} \\\\ \\text{c}∨\\text{c}=\\text{c}\\, &  & \\text{c}∧\\text{c} & =\\text{c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe completed table is shown below.\n\n| Statement | Is Logically Equivalent To |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\text{t}∧\\text{c}$ | $\\text{c}$ |\n| $\\text{c}∨\\text{c}$ | $\\text{c}$ |\n| $\\text{c}∧¬P$ | $\\text{c}$ |\n| $P∨\\text{c}$ | $P$ |" 13194139534155 17592186045417